3.text:
In the $x$–direction put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx ,\qquad
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
W'=-kU ,\qquad 
kP+\mu\,(U''-k^{2}U)=0,\qquad P'=\mu\,(W''-k^{2}W),
\end{equation*}
i.e. $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}W=0$.  With the Fourier mode

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=2A\sinh kz+Bz e^{kz}+Dz e^{-kz},\qquad 
U=-\frac{W'}{k}
\end{equation*}
and $W(0)=0,\;W'(0)=0$ the top is

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)+2\mu W'(0)=-\mu\frac{W'''(0)}{k^{2}}
     =\rho g e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $e$ constant in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=-4\mu kA=-\rho g e,\qquad 
e=\frac{4\mu k}{\rho g}A .\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;P(h)=2\mu W'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu[-kW(h)+U'(h)]=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
which with $W'=-kU$ give

\begin{equation*}
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h),\qquad
 -k^{2}W(h)-W''(h)=\frac{kS_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\alpha=k h$, $a=e^{\alpha}$, $b=e^{-\alpha}$ and
$c=(a+b)/2=\cosh\alpha$,

\begin{equation*}
B/A=-\frac{c+\alpha b}{h c},\qquad 
D/A=k\!\left[-2+\frac1\alpha+\frac{b}{c}\right] ,
\end{equation*}
and the shear condition gives

\begin{equation*}
M\equiv 2ks+B a(1+\alpha)+Db(\alpha-1)
      =-k\Big[\frac{a+b}{\alpha}+\frac{4\alpha}{a+b}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
A=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu M}
   =\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu k}\Big[\frac{a+b}{\alpha}
      +\frac{4\alpha}{a+b}\Big]^{-1} .
\end{equation*}
Using (1)

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \Big[\frac{a+b}{\alpha}+\frac{4\alpha}{a+b}\Big]^{-1}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
With $a+b=2\cosh\alpha$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{k h\,\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}}  ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the form $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rho}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\;
            \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
\alpha=k h,
\end{equation*}
and for the usual geodynamic notation $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
$\rho/\Delta\rho\to1$,

\begin{equation*}
\;\;
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
                 {\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
           =\frac{\alpha}
                  {\cosh\alpha+\alpha^{2}\!\sec h\alpha}\;,
\qquad\alpha=k h\;,
\end{equation*}
 i.e. up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor

$e_{s}= \chi\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  In particular

\[
\chi\simeq\alpha=k h\qquad \alpha\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\sim\alpha\,\hbox{sech}\,\alpha\to0\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

so that for long waves $k h\ll1$

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{k h}{\rho g}\,S_{0}
       \approx\frac{k h}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},
\]

the familiar $e_{s}\propto k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ dynamic
topography of a viscous plate driven by a basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$.  With the $\rho/\Delta\rho$ notation made
explicit this is $e_{s}= (\rho/\Delta\rho)\,\alpha\cosh\alpha/
\big[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}\big]\,S_{0}/\rho g$, which for
$\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$ is the $\chi$ quoted above.  This is the
steady viscous dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal
traction.

1.text:
For a creeping flow put $\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$,

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\psi_{z}=\Phi^{\prime}\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-\psi_{x}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}-\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}\Big],\qquad 
\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}-2k^{2}\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $(\partial _{z}^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$.  With $a_{1}=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a_{2}z\cosh kz+\big(b_{1}+b_{2}z\big)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the material surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi^{\prime}(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{z}=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a_{1}=0\;,\;a_{2}+k b_{1}=0$, and with
$\sigma_{xz}=\mu(\Phi^{\prime\prime}+k^{2}\Phi)\sin kx$, $\sigma_{zz}
=[-P-2\mu k\Phi^{\prime}]\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow \Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(h)=3k^{2}\Phi^{\prime}(h),
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\Longleftrightarrow 
\Phi^{\prime\prime}(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $b_{1}=-a_{2}/k$ the two basal conditions give

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}\Big[\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\Big]=k h\cosh kh\,b_{2},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
2k\cosh kh\Big[\,b_{2}+k h a_{2}\Big]+2k^{2}h\,b_{2}\sinh kh
=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b_{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}= \frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\,
\frac{h\cosh kh}
{\,2\Big[\,\big(\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh\big)
\big(\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big)+k^{2}h^{2}\cosh^{2}kh\Big]}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\,
\frac{h\cosh kh}{2\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]}\; .
\end{equation*}
On the top $u_{x}=0$ so that

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-\,P(0)=\rho g e_{s},\qquad 
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0),
\end{equation*}
and $\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)=2k^{2}a_{2}$.  Hence on the steady
state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k\rho g}\Phi^{\prime\prime\prime}(0)
      =\frac{2\mu k a_{2}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\Delta\rho$ the density contrast at the free surface

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}
       =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}
                +\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}}\ge0 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of the imposed basal traction.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\Big[\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}+\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}\Big]^{-1}
\end{equation*}
so that for $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh\Big[\,1-k^{2}h^{2}+O(k^{4}h^{4})\Big],
\qquad e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $kh\to0$ and for $kh\to\infty$,
$\chi\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$, the maximum being $\chi_{\max}\approx0.5$
around $kh\approx1$.  With the usual sign convention $e_{s}>0$ for
upward deflection this is often written as $e_{s}= -\,\chi S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $|\chi|$ as above.  This is the familiar
shear–induced dynamic topography of a viscous channel.  For a thin
plate $kh\ll1$ the often quoted long–wave limit $\chi\approx k h$ is
recovered. ∎

