3.text:
In the creeping limit ${\rm div}~u=0$, $Re=0$

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$D=d/dz$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0,\qquad 
P=\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[k^{2}\Phi'-\Phi'''\Big],
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and the regular material top $u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\Phi(z)=a_{2}z\cosh kz+\big(b_{1}+b_{2}z\big)\sinh kz,
\qquad b_{1}=-a_{2}/k .
\end{equation*}
Hence $\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}a_{2}$ and with the free top
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e(t)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0),\qquad 
\rho g e_{s}=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0) ,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
on the steady state $\dot e=0$.  On the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
the $\sin kx$ mode automatically satisfying $u_{x}=0,\;\sigma_{xz}=0$
on $x=0,L=\lambda/2$.  With $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ put $\alpha=kh$.
(2) gives, up to the conventional sign of the mode,

\begin{equation*}
a_{2}= \frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\,
      \frac{h\cosh\alpha}{2\big[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}\big]},\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}a_{2},
\end{equation*}
so that from (1)

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
           =S_{0}\,
             \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad
\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $\rho$–formulation

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       f(\alpha),\qquad 
f(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
               {\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
        =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
              +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1}\ge0 .
\end{equation*}
The $\cos kx$ topography is $180^{\circ}$ out of phase with the imposed
$\sin kx$ basal shear, $e_{s}\propto -S_{0}$, so that with the usual
upward positive convention

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= -\,f(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
|e_{s}|=f(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
and the $\sin kx$ mode satisfies the side–wall conditions.

Writing the restoring buoyancy at the free surface as $\Delta\rho g$
and putting $\rho g\to\Delta\rho g$ in the normal stress condition the
geodynamic magnitude is

\begin{equation*}
|e_{s}|=\chi_{0}(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
 =\Bigg[\frac{\cosh\alpha}{\alpha}
       +\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha}\Bigg]^{-1},\qquad\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional crest/trough sign

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=-\,\chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Formally with the $\rho$ used in $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ kept
distinct from the net buoyancy,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
       f(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},
\end{equation*}
which with the usual geodynamic identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
gives $\chi=\chi_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long-wave thin–plate shear dynamic topography, and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\to\infty,
\end{equation*}
with $|\chi_{0}|_{\max}\simeq0.45\!-\!0.50$ at $kh\simeq1.0\!-\!1.2$,
so that $|e_{s}|\approx {\rm const}\times S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for
order–unity modes.  With the $-\,{\rm sign}$ removed this is the quoted
kernel $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi(kh)=
kh\cosh kh\big/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$, i.e. 
$\chi=[\cosh kh/kh+kh/\cosh kh]^{-1}$, up to the conventional
$180^{\circ}$ phase $e_{s}\to -e_{s}$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ prefactor
is unity under the conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification. ∎

